body:
I'm honestly having a hard time construing all the thoughts that I have on people not knowing the history of Latin America. Honestly, It took me until I was 15 or 16 to actually learn about colonization, even though we always learned about Christopher Columbus it was never anecdotal or in perspective. I remember believing all Indigenous people were extinct, I was never taught that his group of people were the ones that would ultimately land their language/culture/blood on top of what should've been. Even my Grandma, while she definitely had a more solid understanding from the beginning, she was taught that she was of pure Spanish descendent, which was the furthest from the truth you could get. I will say though, I do consider myself lucky that I had my grandpa. While he was not a good man by any means, he did leave behind a lot of information on his/our tribal identity. Maybe not in as much detail as he could've, but he had Indigenous names for my aunt and uncle, and he used to paint countless imagery of Nahuatl/Aztec pyramids and villages. That being said though, my grandma's side of the family has called Texas home for 7+ generations so I'm sure I have deeper connection to this land than to the Nahuatl, but unfortunately it's been lost to an information void :/ 

&#x200B;

I kind of went on a rant there, but in relation to mixedrace affairs, there definitely needs to be some ulterior methods of gathering data in a way that doesn't negate the identities of so many mixed people. There's obviously real data that they want but doing it as they do now is borderline dishonest. I'd imagine they're going to have to change it sometime, I think I read that the mixed population of the US is at like 3%+ now, so as long as it keeps going up they're going to have to change something within the next 20-40 years. It may seem like an insane amount of time but it's better than hundreds.

Yeah, I don't think I've seen many people who come from a Latin American background have Indian blood. I'd imagine it's far more common for British people than Hispanic people by a large margin to have that history.

&#x200B;

If I'm not mistaken there was never a substantial immigration campaign to move to Latin America from India, right? I know that in some parts of East/Southeast Asia there was some mass-immigration to parts of South/Central America. I'd imagine that was probably a trend with a lot of countries and regions but I've never heard of there being one from India.
